{"summary": "On 2026-10-24 a fault was injected on purpose into a payments system running on one machine, with synthetic data and a simulated provider, so no real money moved. The fault was a deploy of payments-api with CHARGE_AMOUNT_UNIT=dollars. It made the provider charge customers 100 times the amount our records kept. The alert MoneyCheckFailed fired 17.4 s after the fault. Klaxon investigated and named the cause, and the grading rule agreed. Klaxon asked at the approval gate to redeploy payments-api with CHARGE_AMOUNT_UNIT=cents. The request r-742c was approved, and the action ran as deploy 22. Verification by code passed. The cause was removed and the stack was healthy, but the money was not right. The last money check still found 1080 payments that disagreed with the provider's charges. That damage stays until a person repairs it.", "timeline": "At 08:27:00 UTC the alert rules were quiet at 2 evaluations in a row, and the money check passed. The fault's injection then began. The deploy log has it as deploy 21 of payments-api, by klaxon.faults, at 08:27:03 UTC, and it took 2.4 s. At 08:27:18 UTC the alert MoneyCheckFailed fired. At 08:27:19 UTC Klaxon's loop began to investigate. At 08:27:51 UTC the loop stopped on diagnosed, after 6 steps and 5 tool calls, in 32.5 s. At 08:27:52 UTC Klaxon made request r-742c at the gate, open until 08:37:52 UTC. It was approved at 08:33:15 UTC, after 323 s. The action ran at 08:33:18 UTC as deploy 22. Health checks ran from 08:33:19 UTC to 08:33:35 UTC and found the stack healthy. A later money check ran at 08:34:18 UTC. Verification passed at 438.1 s after the fault. The harness's clearing followed at 438.3 s, found the good settings already running, and changed nothing. At 08:35:49 UTC the rules read the stack again, and MoneyCheckFailed fired.", "impact": "At the alert the money check failed 1 of its 5 invariants, payments_match_provider, with 56 findings. Ledger and payment-confirmer lag were 0. Right before the request, 149 payments disagreed with the provider's charges. Of those, 145 had a differing amount, and 4 were pending but charged with a differing amount. Right after the action, 1080 payments disagreed. Of those, 1060 had a differing amount, and 20 were pending but charged with a differing amount. The damage grew while the request waited for approval. The later check at 08:34:18 UTC and the reading after the clearing both still showed 1080. The first examples began at pmt_000230. The synthetic clients sent 3 new payments per second on average. 1791 payments arrived and all 1791 got an answer. 1771 succeeded, 0 were refused, and 20 were left with their outcome unknown and still pending at the client's last status check. Refunds: 167 succeeded and 0 were refused. The clients retried 98 times. The evidence does not say whether the 20 pending payments are the same ones the money check listed as pending but charged.", "cause": "The recorded cause was a deploy of the payments API with CHARGE_AMOUNT_UNIT=dollars. It read each amount, already in cents, as dollars and converted it to cents again. So the provider charged every customer 100 times the amount, while our own records kept the right one. The payments then disagreed with the provider's charges, and that failed the payments_match_provider invariant, which raised MoneyCheckFailed. Klaxon's diagnosis, with confidence 0.85, said payments-api had been redeployed with CHARGE_AMOUNT_UNIT=dollars and the provider was charging 100 times what we recorded. A rule in klaxon.grading, not a person or a model, judged that it names the cause. It found every required element named, and found that the fix fits the remedy. The rule reads words, not meaning. The diagnosis gave a deploy time that differs from the deploy log's time. It also claimed the ledger still matches the provider, which code did not check.", "response": "Klaxon had tools that only read, plus one write action, rollback_deploy, which runs only if a person approves it. It asked at the gate, as request r-742c, to redeploy payments-api with CHARGE_AMOUNT_UNIT changed from dollars to cents and the other settings unchanged. The request's undo said going back to dollars would deploy a known-bad setting, which Klaxon will not do, though a person can by hand. The request was approved with make approve, typed at a terminal. The gate's log records how a decision was made, not who made it. The rule is that only Aravind approves. The action ran as deploy 22, with PROVIDER_BREAKER=on, CHARGE_AMOUNT_UNIT=cents and REFUND_CONNECTION=returned. Verification by code passed. Every container was healthy and the rules were quiet at 2 evaluations in a row. Nothing the provider made after the action was booked wrong, so the cause was removed. The money was not right. The facts do not explain why the alert fired again after the clearing, while verification had found the rules quiet.", "repairs": "The last money check, at 08:34:18 UTC, still found 1080 payments that disagree with the provider's charges. A rollback repairs nothing. Repairs that move money are not among Klaxon's actions, so a person must make them. The recorded remedy is to refund each customer charged since the deploy what was charged above the amount, at the provider. Our API refunds no more than our recorded amount, so this needs a write with approval. The 20 payments still pending at the client's last status check also need a person's attention. The evidence does not say whether each has settled since. The damaged payments the check lists need the repair the fault records, not a retry or being marked failed."}
```

